<div class="breadcrumb"><a href="../index.html">Home</a><span>›</span><a href="index.html">Blog</a><span>›</span>How to Choose a Drywall Contractor</div>
<div class="post-layout">
<article class="post-article">
<p class="post-intro">Choosing the wrong drywall contractor is an expensive mistake. Redoing poor drywall after painting costs significantly more than paying for quality the first time. In Oakville's custom home market, a bad finish reflects on everyone involved. Here is how to tell the difference.</p>

<img loading="lazy" class="post-img" src="../images/custom1.jpeg" alt="Quality drywall work custom home coffered ceiling Oakville" style="height:340px;object-fit:cover;">

<h2>1. Ask to See Recent Work — In Person if Possible</h2>
<p>Any drywall contractor worth hiring can show you recent projects. Photos are a starting point but they hide what matters — how joints hold up under raking light, the quality of corner bead, the consistency of finish across large wall surfaces. A site visit to a completed project (with homeowner permission) tells you more in five minutes than any photo portfolio.</p>

<h2>2. Confirm They Use Their Own Crew</h2>
<p>Look for contractors with consistent, long-term teams. Ask how long they have worked with their installers and tapers. Continuity and experience within a team produces far more consistent results than rotating workers.</p>

<img loading="lazy" class="post-img" src="../images/d3.jpeg" alt="Commercial drywall framing — Halton Region" style="height:280px;object-fit:cover;">

<h2>3. Get an Itemized Quote</h2>
<p>A quote that says "drywall complete — $18,000" tells you nothing. An itemized quote shows you the finish level included, number of coats, whether sanding is included, cleanup, and what happens with unforeseen framing issues. Itemized quotes protect both parties and eliminate confusion at the end of the job.</p>

<h2>4. Verify WSIB Coverage and Insurance</h2>
<p>Every drywall contractor working in Ontario must have WSIB (Workplace Safety and Insurance Board) coverage. Ask for a clearance certificate — it takes 30 seconds to verify online at wsib.ca. Also confirm current commercial general liability insurance. If a worker is injured on your property and the contractor is not covered, you could be held liable.</p>

<h2>5. Ask Specifically About Finish Level</h2>
<p>Most homeowners do not know the difference between Level 4 and Level 5 finish. Many contractors quote Level 4 because it is faster and cheaper — then the homeowner discovers visible joints under paint in certain lighting. Ask directly: what level of finish are you quoting? For any Oakville home with quality paint, the answer should be Level 5.</p>

<h2>6. Watch for These Red Flags</h2>
<ul>
  <li>No written quote — verbal agreements only</li>
  <li>Large cash deposit required upfront (more than 20%)</li>
  <li>Cannot provide references or examples of similar work</li>
  <li>Price significantly below all other quotes</li>
  <li>No verifiable business history or physical address</li>
  <li>Pressure to decide immediately</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout callout-warn">
  <strong>The reality on pricing</strong>
  <p>If you are investing $800,000 to $2M+ on a custom home in Oakville, drywall is not the place to save $3,000. A flawless Level 5 finish adds value to your home. Visible joints under satin paint take it away — and remind you of the decision every time you walk past.</p>
</div>

<h2>7. Check Google Reviews and Homestars</h2>
<p>A pattern of detailed positive reviews mentioning high quality, timeliness, clean sites and professional crews is a strong signal. Be cautious of contractors with no reviews, heavily generic reviews, or mentions of delays, callbacks and touch-ups.</p>

